---
/**
 * The privacy policy.
 *
 * Written as a description of what the software actually does, because it was
 * written next to the software that does it: every claim on this page can be
 * checked against a file in this repository, and the account page shows a
 * signed-in reader the specific values rather than the categories. If the code
 * changes, this changes with it — a policy that drifts from the system it
 * describes is worse than none, because people rely on it.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Logo from "../components/Logo.astro";
import { CAL, CAL_READY, EMAIL, PROVIDER_HOST, ROUTES, SIGN_IN_READY } from "../lib/auth/config";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const UPDATED = "14 September 2026";
const calHost = (() => {
  try {
    return new URL(CAL.origin).host;
  } catch {
    return CAL.origin;
  }
})();
---

<Base
  title="Privacy — OmniDynamics"
  description="What OmniDynamics collects, who processes it, and how to get rid of it."
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="top">
      <a class="brand clicky" href={withBase("/")}>
        <Logo size={30} />
        <span class="wordmark">OmniDynamics</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="links">
        {SIGN_IN_READY && <a class="clicky" href={ROUTES.account}>Your account</a>}
        <a class="clicky" href={ROUTES.book}>Book a demo</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        What this site collects, who else sees it, and how to get rid of it.
      </p>
      <p class="stamp">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>

      <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

      <h2>Reading the site</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing is collected. There is no analytics script, no tag manager, no
        advertising pixel and no cookie of ours on any page — the site is a set
        of static files and a shader. Our host, Cloudflare, keeps ordinary
        server logs of requests, including IP addresses, to serve the site and
        keep it up; we do not build profiles from them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 3D wall runs on your own graphics hardware. It measures nothing
        about you and sends nothing anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>Signing in</h2>
      {SIGN_IN_READY ? (
        <>
          <p>
            Signing in is <strong>Sign in with Google</strong>, and that is the
            whole of it. You authenticate at
            {" "}<span class="host">{PROVIDER_HOST}</span>, not here. Your
            password, one-time code or passkey never touches this site, and we
            could not read it if we tried.
          </p>
          <p>
            What comes back is one identity token: a short statement, signed by
            Google, saying who you are. It carries <strong>your name</strong>,
            {" "}<strong>your email address</strong>, your profile picture and
            the identifier Google uses for you, and your account page lists
            every field of it. It is <em>not</em> an access token — there is no
            key to your mail, your files, your calendar or your contacts in it,
            we did not ask for one, and there is nothing here that could be used
            to act as you at Google.
          </p>
          <p>
            We check that token before believing a word of it: the signature
            against Google's published keys, that Google issued it, that it was
            issued to this site rather than some other one, that it answers the
            one-time value this page generated, and that it has not expired. A
            token that fails any of those is thrown away.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is kept in this tab's storage and nowhere else. Closing the tab
            discards it, it expires on its own within the hour, and nothing goes
            to <code>localStorage</code>, where it would outlive the visit. We
            have no server that could store it, because we have no server.
          </p>
          <p>
            Because Google is who you sign in with, Google knows that you use
            this site, in the same way it would for any site you sign in to with
            it. What Google does with that is governed by
            {" "}<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noreferrer">its
            own privacy policy</a>, and you can withdraw this site's access at
            any time from
            {" "}<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections" rel="noreferrer">your
            Google account's connections</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you sign in to two different accounts on one browser, anything
            cached for the first is deleted the moment the second arrives. We
            key that cache on the issuer <em>and</em> your identifier, so two
            people with the same identifier at different providers are never the
            same person here.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          Sign-in is not enabled on this build of the site, so there is no
          account, no token and nothing stored.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>Booking a demo</h2>
      {CAL_READY ? (
        <>
          <p>
            The booking form is <a href="https://cal.com/">Cal.com</a>, an
            open-source scheduler, embedded from
            {" "}<span class="host">{calHost}</span>. It loads only when you open
            the booking page — not on any other page of this site — so nothing
            from Cal.com runs while you are simply reading.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you type into it goes to Cal.com and to us: your name, your
            email address, the time you picked and anything you write in the
            notes. If you are already signed in, your name and address are
            filled in for you and remain editable. We use it to hold the call
            and to follow up about it, and for nothing else.
          </p>
          <p>
            The call itself is held on <strong>Cal Video</strong>, Cal.com's own
            video room. The confirmation carries that link, and joining it needs
            no account and no download — the meeting runs in your browser.
            There is no Google Meet, no Zoom and no third meeting provider in
            the middle of it: the only company handling the call is the one that
            handled the booking.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          Booking is not enabled on this build. Mail sent to the address below
          reaches us directly.
        </p>
      )}

      <h2>Who else processes it</h2>
      <ul class="who">
        <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> — serves the site; request logs.</li>

        {CAL_READY && (
          <li>
            <strong>{calHost}</strong> — Cal.com; scheduling, confirmations, and
            the Cal Video room the call is held in.
          </li>
        )}
        {SIGN_IN_READY && (
          <li>
            <strong>Google</strong> — authenticates you and tells us your name
            and email address. That is its whole part in this: it is not in the
            booking, and it is not in the call.
          </li>
        )}
      </ul>
      <p class="thin">
        That is the whole list. We do not sell anything to anyone, and there is
        no fourth party you have not been told about.
      </p>

      <h2>How long</h2>
      <p>
        The identity token: until it expires, or the tab closes — whichever
        comes first. Cached page state: until you sign out, or a different
        account signs in on that browser. Bookings and the mail around them:
        kept while we are in touch, and deleted on request.
      </p>

      <h2>Your say</h2>
      <p>
        You can see what is held, clear it, and sign out from
        {SIGN_IN_READY ? <> <a href={ROUTES.account}>your account page</a></> : <> the account page, once sign-in is enabled</>}.
        For access, correction, export or deletion of anything beyond the
        browser — a booking, a thread of mail, the account itself — write to
        {" "}<a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> and we will do it and tell
        you what was removed. If you are in the UK or the EU, those are your
        rights under the GDPR and you do not have to cite it to use them; you
        may also complain to your data protection authority.
      </p>
      <p class="thin">
        This site is not for children, and we do not knowingly collect anything
        from anyone under 16.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        This page is versioned alongside the code it describes. Material changes
        move the date at the top, and anything that widens what we collect will
        be asked for rather than announced.
      </p>

      <p class="back"><a class="clicky" href={withBase("/")}>Back to the site</a></p>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-block-size: 100dvh;
    max-inline-size: 48rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem) clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.5rem)
      clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5rem);
    color: var(--on-wall);
  }

  .top {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-block-end: clamp(2rem, 6vh, 3.5rem);
  }
  .brand {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    border-radius: 999px;
  }
  .wordmark { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .links { display: flex; gap: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  .links a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; opacity: 0.62; transition: opacity 0.2s ease; }
  .links a:hover { opacity: 1; }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .lede {
    margin: clamp(0.9rem, 2.5vh, 1.4rem) 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2.2vw, 1.5rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
    font-weight: 300;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .stamp {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .rule {
    inline-size: min(100%, 22rem);
    block-size: 1px;
    margin-block: clamp(1.5rem, 4vh, 2.5rem);
    background: currentColor;
    opacity: 0.28;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: clamp(2rem, 5vh, 2.75rem) 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    max-inline-size: 38rem;
  }
  p:last-of-type { margin-block-end: 0; }
  .thin { font-size: 0.9375rem; color: var(--muted); }

  a { color: inherit; text-underline-offset: 0.22em; }
  .host, strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  code {
    font-size: 0.85em;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 0.35em;
    background: rgb(60 48 34 / 0.08);
  }

  .who {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .back {
    margin-block-start: clamp(2.5rem, 7vh, 4rem);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
